{:edb/db-before-t 2171 :edb/db-after-t 2172 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535484 50 #inst "2026-10-14T04:23:19.020Z" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338762 1074 "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $M$ by the $4$th column in $N$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{34} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 7 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-1)+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =0-4+21+30 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338762 1074 "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $M$ by the $4$th column in $N$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{34} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-1)+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =0-4+21+30 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338765 1071 "Find $Tu$ if \n$T=[\\begin{aligned}x & y & x \\\\ x & -y & x \\\\ -x & -y & x\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338765 1071 "Find $Tu$ if \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}x & y & x \\\\ x & -y & x \\\\ -x & -y & x\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$u=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338765 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tu & =[\\begin{aligned}x & y & x \\\\ x & -y & x \\\\ -x & -y & x\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot x+y\\cdot 0+x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot x+(-y)\\cdot 0+x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ (-x)\\cdot x+(-y)\\cdot 0+x\\cdot (-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+0-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+0-{x}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338765 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tu & =\\begin{bmatrix}x & y & x \\\\ x & -y & x \\\\ -x & -y & x\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\cdot x+y\\cdot 0+x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot x+(-y)\\cdot 0+x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ (-x)\\cdot x+(-y)\\cdot 0+x\\cdot (-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+0-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+0-{x}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338798 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-5t \\\\ 2+t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5t \\\\ t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338798 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-5t \\\\ 2+t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5t \\\\ t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338806 1071 "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338806 1071 "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338806 1074 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ t \\\\ {t}^{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ {t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338806 1074 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t \\\\ {t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ {t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338810 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ 3-t \\\\ t+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(1,3,2)$  and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338810 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t+1 \\\\ 3-t \\\\ t+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(1,3,2)$  and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338812 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}t-1 \\\\ -t+1 \\\\ -t-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-1,1,-1)$   and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338812 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}t-1 \\\\ -t+1 \\\\ -t-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-1,1,-1)$   and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338813 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}t+1 \\\\ 5-\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(1,5,\\frac{1}{3})$  and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338813 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}t+1 \\\\ 5-\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(1,5,\\frac{1}{3})$  and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338826 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338826 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186338827 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}-2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{2} \\\\ 2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186338827 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}-2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\