ents.\n\nLet $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186342030 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186342030 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 true] [17592186342030 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535484 17592186045443 false] [17592186342030 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 